-- A LIST after `BREACH BY` names several parties, one per element, unless
-- the rule's party type is itself that list type, in which case it names ONE
-- party whose value is a list. Which reading is meant is decided by the party
-- type, so a BREACH reached with nothing upstream fixing its party type
-- cannot decide it, and is refused HERE, at the BREACH. Guessing the element
-- reading instead pinned the party type to the element type and failed
-- later, at the use site, naming the wrong place, and passed with the RAND's
-- operands swapped. (From unstable's 9d620c355, EVERY-EACH-QUANTIFIER-SPEC
-- §6.1.1 there.)
--
-- Shape 1 is fixed by a GIVETH on the definition. Shapes 2 and 3 are fixed by
-- writing the PARTY operand first. Shape 3 is accepted on unstable, which
-- carries the GIVETH's type into both operands of a RAND; here it does not
-- reach the first operand.

DECLARE Step IS ONE OF deliver

-- 1. A top-level MEANS with no GIVETH: nothing says the party type.
`the pair fails` MEANS
    BREACH BY (LIST "a", "b") BECAUSE "pair"

-- 2. The first operand of a RAND with no GIVETH: the compound is inferred
--    with a fresh party type, and the first operand is checked before the
--    second (the PARTY) has fixed it.
`pair` MEANS
    (BREACH BY (LIST "a", "b") BECAUSE "pair")
    RAND
    (PARTY (LIST "a", "b") MUST deliver WITHIN 5)

-- 3. The same under a GIVETH: the GIVETH does not reach the first operand.
GIVETH A DEONTIC STRING Step
`pair with a signature` MEANS
    (BREACH BY (LIST "a", "b") BECAUSE "pair")
    RAND
    (PARTY "a" MUST deliver WITHIN 5)
